# Cloudflare Pages redirects for the sparrowmap.com static site.
#
# 🚨 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: sparrowmap.com/status returned 200 and served the
# LANDING PAGE. Pages falls back to index.html for any path it does not have,
# so a link that looks right, answers 200, and shows the wrong page - which is
# the worst of the three, because nothing about it looks broken.
#
# /status is the page people are sent to when someone claims the site is
# blocked, and sparrowmap.com is the domain they will type. A status link that
# quietly shows the marketing page instead would lose exactly the argument it
# was built to win.
#
# 302 rather than 301: the status page lives on the hub today, and a permanent
# redirect is cached by browsers for a long time and is painful to take back if
# that ever moves.
/status  https://map.sparrowmap.com/status  302

# The same trap, for the two paths people already share by hand.
/map     https://map.sparrowmap.com/        302
/app     https://map.sparrowmap.com/app     302

# Sparrow Send. sparrowmap.com/send is what people will type - it is shorter than
# map.sparrowmap.com/send and it is the domain on the cards and the nav.
#
# 🚨 A REDIRECT, NEVER A PROXY, and this one matters more than the others.
# The Send app keeps the whole identity in localStorage: the private keys, the
# contacts, the Double Ratchet state and the seen-message ids. localStorage is
# per-ORIGIN. If sparrowmap.com/send SERVED the app instead of redirecting, that
# would be a second origin with its own empty store - the user would look logged
# out on a domain they typed correctly, could mint a duplicate identity, and would
# register a second /send service worker. Two origins would also mean two devices
# on one mailbox, and the relay deletes on read, so whichever polled first would
# steal the message. Keep exactly ONE origin for the app: map.sparrowmap.com.
#
# The splat sends /send/anything to the app rather than to a 404 - the app has no
# subpaths of its own, so there is nothing to preserve.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT IS ACTUALLY SERVING THIS TODAY: a zone Redirect Rule, not this file.
# This Pages project is DIRECT UPLOAD, not git-connected (source: null, deploys are
# ad_hoc), so pushing this repo does NOT deploy the landing site - it has to be sent
# with `wrangler pages deploy landing --project-name=sparrowmap`. Rather than hand-
# roll a direct upload of the whole site just to add one line (a botched upload takes
# sparrowmap.com down), /send is live as a Cloudflare Redirect Rule on zone
# 24d1f244aeb1df46303a7dfc9beaa962, ruleset 2465c0bfe895420d91301aa283a21d70,
# phase http_request_dynamic_redirect, named "SparrowMap apex redirects".
# It is scoped to apex+www ONLY - a zone-wide /send match would also catch
# map.sparrowmap.com/send and redirect it to itself forever.
# These two lines are kept anyway so this file stays the honest description of the
# routing, and they become the live mechanism the next time the site is deployed.
# Same destination and status either way, so the two cannot disagree.
/send    https://map.sparrowmap.com/send    302
/send/*  https://map.sparrowmap.com/send    302
